  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nWe find ourselves sandwiched between a pair of prominent \nfeatures synoptically... closed, retrograding low off Atlantic \nCanada will allow for the amplification of 500mb ridging aloft,\nand thus leading to increased heights and subsidence aloft for a\nfew days. At the surface, backdoor frontal passage well underway\nas drier air builds in from the northeast... dewpoints in the \n50s have infiltrated eastern upper and are on the move into far \nnorthern and northeast lower, while the rest of the area has \nseen dewpoints settle near or just above 60... the lone exception\nbeing the far SW of the CWA, which continues to be in a juicer...\nTVC sitting at 66 Td, CAD holding with a 68 Td, and MBL clinging\non to a 71 Td. As long as this amount of moisture remains in \nplace, suppose a lingering shower could drag its feet into the \nmorning hours toward Frankfort / Manistee / Cadillac, but that \nshould be short-lived. Stationary front itself has migrated well\nsouth... stretching from roughly La Crosse, WI through Chicago \nand ESE through Pittsburgh and Philadelphia. As such, \naforementioned retrograding low off the Atlantic will continue \nto drive the front SSW, and support a period of drier, tranquil \nlate-summer weather for the remainder of the holiday weekend. \nRidge axis overhead paired with increasingly deep SW return flow\nwill drive temperatures 5-10 degrees above average into the \nbeginning of the week (highs generally 73-83 today through \nTuesday, perhaps a touch warmer Wednesday).\n\nAnother shortwave surges into the Pacific Northwest early this \nweek... perhaps bringing a dandy of an enhanced moisture plume \nfrom eastern Pacific Hurricane Marie... and drawing all that \nmoisture over the ridge axis. Core of the wave and associated \nsurface cyclogenesis looks to eject into the Dakotas and \nCanadian Prairies / NW Ontario by midweek, but will force a \nfront into the region on Wednesday. Coupling this with potential\nfor high humidity and (pending arrival of best forcing) \ninstability, this will likely bring about the next shower / \nstorm chances... with perhaps a shot at some heavy rain. Still a\nlot of variables to sort out before proceeding with messaging \nexplicitly outlining heavy rain potential, so will probably ride\nwith generic \"shower and thunder chances\" verbiage until this \nsystem actually moves into North America. Long- range guidance \nseems to want to turn resultant flow aloft to more zonal with \nthis system's passage... which essentially translates to a \nstalling frontal boundary across the Great Lakes region. Current\nindications are that this front looks to stall just to our \nsouth to close out the week. This conceptually would result in \nmore of an unsettled, \"cooler\" but probably still somewhat humid\nairmass for a few days to end the week and going into next \nweekend.",
